WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.760
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: With over
300 sessions at Next 2018,

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.710
it's understandable you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.460
So if you didn't see
"How to Do Predictive

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.320
Analytics in BigQuery,"
then stay tuned,

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.403
because here's the rewind.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.006
You can now do machine
learning right in BigQuery.

00:00:19.006 --> 00:00:20.880
And you're about to see
what that looks like.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.050
But first, what's
all the fuss about?

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:26.750
BigQuery is Google Cloud's
enterprise-class data

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:27.440
warehouse.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.260
If you have petabytes of data,
you can analyze all of it

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.240
with a single query
in standard SQL.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.310
And you can even do
real-time analytics.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:36.890
The best part is that BigQuery
is serverless and fully

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.290
managed, which means that
all you need to think about

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:41.819
is what data you have and
how you want to analyze it.

00:00:41.819 --> 00:00:43.610
Machine learning is an
algorithmic approach

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.320
to making repeated
decisions with data.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.090
And it's incredibly useful.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.550
It lets you automate
whole new classes of tasks

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.570
and create business
critical prediction systems.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:55.070
To make machine learning work,
you need data and algorithms.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:58.950
Putting them together gives you
models for making predictions.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.390
So if you were waiting for
the catch, there it is.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:03.920
This little thing
about putting them

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.190
together can actually be
much harder than it sounds.

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:08.810
Data fuels machine learning.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:10.400
It's what makes
machine learning tick.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.095
And you have it in BigQuery.

00:01:12.095 --> 00:01:14.720
Now you've got to get your data
into machine learning software.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.280
And your data set is not small.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.260
How do you get a copy
of your whole beast

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:21.200
of a multi-petabyte data
set into TensorFlow in order

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.115
to get that sophisticated
model you're after?

00:01:23.115 --> 00:01:25.490
Now you're back to thinking
about infrastructure at scale

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:26.720
again, which is
what you were trying

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.140
to avoid in the first place.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.290
So instead of bringing your
data to machine learning,

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.370
you can help bring machine
learning to your data

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:34.730
right in BigQuery.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.442
That's what BigQuery
ML is all about.

00:01:36.442 --> 00:01:38.400
And it supports linear
and logistic regression.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.170
So that means forecasting and
classification just got easy.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:42.920
Here's what housing
prediction looks like.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.260
And as an added bonus, you
get those model evaluation

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.950
functions you know and love,
including the ROC curve.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.374
What you're looking at here
is the Data Studio version.

00:01:51.374 --> 00:01:52.790
We've got Looker
integration, too,

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:54.470
making it easy to
explore data, create

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:57.470
models, and operationalize
your ML workflow with Looker.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:00.020
BigQuery ML means that
machine learning at scale

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.460
is no longer just for huge teams
of data scientists and data

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:04.340
engineers.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:05.810
And the possibilities
are endless.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.268
There are so many
applications just

00:02:07.268 --> 00:02:09.090
waiting for you to dive in.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.840
In the full talk, you'll get
inspired by alpha customer use

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:12.371
cases.

00:02:12.371 --> 00:02:13.870
And you'll hear
directly from Hearst

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.860
as they tell their
BigQuery ML story.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.260
And of course, you'll get
to see BigQuery ML in action

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:19.142
in the live demo.

00:02:19.142 --> 00:02:20.600
If you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.410
check out the link in
the description below.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.493
And don't forget to subscribe
to the Cloud YouTube

00:02:24.493 --> 00:02:26.840
channel for more great
Next rewind content.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:27.770
Thanks for watching.

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:31.720
